WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:10.380
The second component in actions is chips. Chips are small components, very similar to a button. They also give me the option to show the

2
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:18.260
user some data, or to let the user take actions, as we'll see now in our examples, and we'll see how they differ from buttons.

3
00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:30.300
One of the most important differences and uses of chips is that I can put more than one chip in the same area, and the user can interact with them in one area. Unlike buttons: we said there should be one or two buttons, no more

4
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:42.340
than three at most on the same screen, and we need them to be different types so we can prioritize, or give preference to one action: that the user needs to take this action on this page, and that it's the best one, and so on.

5
00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:51.807
But with chips I can use a group, more than one chip in the same place, so the user can take a certain action through them. Like when the user enters the people they want to email: they type

6
00:00:51.807 --> 00:01:01.273
their names or emails, and these appear as chips, and they can tap a chip to remove someone from the email, meaning they no longer want to send it to them. When they type someone else, that person is

7
00:01:01.273 --> 00:01:10.740
added as a chip, and so on. It can easily grow to a large group of chips in the same place. That's one of the most important differences from the button, and one of the main strengths of chips.

8
00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:18.330
The chips we'll usually use come in four types, the ones in front of us: assist, filter, input and

9
00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:25.920
suggestion chips. Each type, of course, has a different use, and these types are widely used in many products.

10
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:36.240
The first type is assist chips: they let us move between different apps or products for certain actions. For example, if I'm looking at a restaurant's page and want to see where it

11
00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:46.560
is, its location on the map, I might have a chip that takes me out of the app to the maps app, where I see the location. Or if I want to book

12
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:56.880
a certain time, so I want to check my calendar, or see certain meetings in it, I might have a chip that takes me to my calendar, shows me my appointments, and where

13
00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:07.200
exactly this one will fit, and so on. So the main job of assist chips is to connect apps, or let us move between apps, going from one to another in a certain scenario.

14
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:17.240
Filter chips almost all of us know and use: they usually sit under the search, for example, or on any page with a set of products, or any

15
00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:27.280
page where I'm browsing. I may have a set of filters as chips; if I tap any of them, it starts applying to the content on the page, filtering

16
00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:37.320
it based on the chip I tapped. We also talked about filters, and chips are among the most common components used in filters, especially in mobile and tablet apps.

17
00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:47.360
There are also examples like an image site: on the left the filter is made of a set of chips, and any chip I tap applies that filter immediately.

18
00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:57.532
That takes us to the next type, the input chip. When I choose something in the filter and it's now applied, it may show up as an input chip. An

19
00:02:57.532 --> 00:03:07.704
input chip is something I entered as a user, shown to me as an input chip, and I can tap it to remove the input I entered. Like the filter example:

20
00:03:07.704 --> 00:03:17.876
I've chosen a filter like Vectors on the image site; if I tap the Vectors chip, that filter is no longer applied. And the example of the people I entered

21
00:03:17.876 --> 00:03:28.048
in an email to send it to them: as soon as I type a name and press Enter, or move on to the next person, it turns into an input chip

22
00:03:28.048 --> 00:03:38.220
automatically, an input I entered, and if I tap it later it cancels that input completely, it deletes it, and so on. So that's the third type of chips, input chips.

23
00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:50.000
The fourth and last type is suggestion chips: they appear to suggest certain actions to me, or anything in a scenario I'm in. Like when I translate a word and there are other similar words: I may

24
00:03:50.000 --> 00:04:01.780
see a set of chips with different words related to the word I'm translating, and I tap any of them to see its translation, and so on. So they're suggestions related to what I already did, or

25
00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:13.560
the scenario I'm in. Another example is the chips that appear in some chats: sometimes when someone sends us a message, a set of chips appears suggesting different replies to it. These are all examples of suggestion chips.

26
00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:25.080
One of the important guidelines for chips in general is having a group in the same place. All the examples, uses and types of chips we covered are used as a group of actions in

27
00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:36.600
the same place. Usually I don't put a single chip and make it do a single action; then it's like a button. That's the difference between chips and buttons: when there's a group in the same

28
00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:48.120
place doing actions related to the types we talked about, I use chips. But when I have one main action, usually not closely tied to the actions we just mentioned, I'll use one component to express

29
00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:59.640
it, and that will be the button. But if I want the user to have different actions related to the ones we just mentioned, chips are the best fit. That's the difference between buttons and chips.

30
00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:11.365
Also, buttons are usually used to take me a step forward or a step back in the flow I'm in, or to let me take a main action, like posting

31
00:05:11.365 --> 00:05:23.090
now, for example, or publishing something, and so on. That's where we use buttons. But chips, as we said, have specific uses, especially in the scenarios and types we just covered.
